L000574	\section{Basics of $S_3$ quantum double model}
L000575	
L000576	For more details, we refer the reader to the comprehensive pedagogical notes in Refs.~\onlinecite{cui_lecture_notes,lo_universal_2025}.
L000577	
L000578	\subsection{Review of $S_3$ group theory and representation theory}\label{append-sub:S3-review}
L000579	
L000580	We use the group presentation for $S_3=\left<\mu,\sigma \vert \mu^3=\sigma^2=e, \mu\sigma = \sigma \mu^{-1} \right>$, where $\mu$ and $\sigma$ corresponds to the $120^\circ$ rotation and reflection of an equilateral triangle. Another common notation is to recognize that $S_3$ is a symmetric group on three elements, so group elements are permutations that can be represented in terms of cycles. The identification is $\mu=(123)$, $\sigma = (23)$ (the remaining group elements can be determined from these two generators).
L000581	
L000582	There are three conjugacy classes that partitions $S_3$: $C_1=\set{e}$, $C_2=\set{\sigma,\mu\sigma,\bar\mu\sigma}$, $C_3=\set{\mu,\bar\mu}$.
L000583	
L000584	There are three irreducible representations (irreps) of $S_3$: $[+],[-],[2]$. The trivial representation $[+]$ sends every group element to $1$. The sign representation $[-]$ sends group element $g \mapsto \text{sgn}(g)$, where $\text{sgn}$ gives the sign of the permutation; so even permutations (e.g. $C_3$ group elements) are mapped to $+1$, whereas odd permutations (e.g. $C_2$ group elements) are mapped to $-1$. The standard representation $[2]$ is a two-dimensional representation, and the representation matrices are:
L000585	\begin{align*}
L000586		\Gamma_{[2]}(e)=\begin{pmatrix}
L000587			1 & 0\\
L000588			0 & 1\\
L000589		\end{pmatrix}\quad\quad
L000590		\Gamma_{[2]}(\mu)&=\begin{pmatrix}
L000591			\omega & 0\\
L000592			0 & \bar\omega\\
L000593		\end{pmatrix}\quad\quad
L000594		\Gamma_{[2]}(\bar\mu)=\begin{pmatrix}
L000595			\bar\omega & 0\\
L000596			0 & \omega\\
L000597		\end{pmatrix}\quad\quad \\
L000598		\Gamma_{[2]}(\mu \sigma)=\begin{pmatrix}
L000599			0 & \omega\\
L000600			\bar\omega & 0\\
L000601		\end{pmatrix}\quad\quad
L000602		\Gamma_{[2]}(\bar\mu \sigma)&=\begin{pmatrix}
L000603			0 & \bar\omega\\
L000604			\omega & 0\\
L000605		\end{pmatrix}\quad\quad
L000606		\Gamma_{[2]}(\sigma)=\begin{pmatrix}
L000607			0 & 1\\
L000608			1 & 0\\
L000609		\end{pmatrix}\quad\quad
L000610	\end{align*}
L000611	where the indexing order is $\{\ket{2+},\ket{2-}\}$ and $\omega = \exp(2 \pi i/3)$.
L000612	
L000613	The representation theory of the $\mathbb{Z}_3$ subgroup of $S_3$ also warrants a mention, as it will be important for defining some of the anyon types in $S_3$ quantum double. Since $\mathbb{Z}_3$ is an Abelian group, all irreps are one-dimensional. The three possible irreps are labeled by the third root of unity. The trivial representation $[1]$ maps all group elements to $1$. The $[\omega]$ representation is given by the map $a \mapsto \omega^a$. The $[\bar{\omega}]$ representation is given by the map $a \mapsto (\bar{\omega})^{a}$.
L000614	
L000615	\subsection{Qutrit-qubit encoding}\label{app:qubit-qutrit-encoding}
L000616	
L000617	We use the qutrit-qubit encoding for the $S_3$ group elements, such that for a given $S_3$ group element basis $\ket{g} = \ket{\mu^a\sigma^b}$, it is encoded as a qutrit $\ket{a \text{ (mod 3)}}$ and a qubit $\ket{b \text{ (mod 2)}}$, where $a,b$ are integers.
L000618	
L000619	
L000620	The left- and right-group multiplication operators are
L000621	\begin{equation}
L000622	    L^g\ket{h} = \ket{gh}, \quad \quad \quad R^g\ket{h} = \ket{h\bar{g}},
L000623	\end{equation}
L000624	where $\bar g$ is the inverse of $g$.
L000625	In terms of qutrit and qubit gates, the left- and right- group multiplication of $S_3$ generators are
L000626	\begin{align}
L000627	    L^\mu &= \mathcal{X}\otimes I\;,\; \quad  L^\sigma = \mathcal{C}\otimes X \;,\;\label{eq:q2q3_enc_lr_multiplication_1}\\
L000628	    R^\mu &= \mathcal{X}^{-Z}\;,\; \quad  R^\sigma = I\otimes X\;,\;\label{eq:q2q3_enc_lr_multiplication_2}
L000629	\end{align}
L000630	where $\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{Z}$ denote the qutrit clock matrices, and $\mathcal{C}$ is the qutrit charge-conjugation operator,
L000631	\begin{equation}\label{eq:x_and_z_and_C_def}
L000632	    \mathcal{Z} = \begin{pmatrix}
L000633	    1 & & \\
L000634	    & \omega & \\
L000635	    & & \omega^2
L000636	    \end{pmatrix}, \quad
L000637	    \mathcal{X} = \begin{pmatrix}
L000638	    &  & 1\\
L000639	    1 & & \\
L000640	    & 1 & \end{pmatrix}, \quad
L000641	    \mathcal{C} = \begin{pmatrix}
L000642			1 & & \\
L000643			&  & 1\\
L000644			& 1 &
L000645		\end{pmatrix}\;,
L000646	\end{equation}
L000647	and where $Z$ and $X$ are the Pauli matrices acting on the qubit degrees of freedom.
L000648	\\
L000649	
L000650	Given an $S_3$ group element $g=\mu^a\sigma^b$, its inverse is $\bar{g} = \mu^{-(-1)^ba}\sigma^b$. This inverse operation is implemented by the following circuit:
L000651	\begin{equation}\label{eq:inverse-circ}
