L000870	\subsubsection{Unitary ribbon}\label{app:unitary-ribbon}
L000871	We now specialize to useful ribbons used in the main text. We observe that by summing over the local internal state on one end of the ribbon, the resulting ribbon operator is unitary. Such unitary ribbon can be decomposed into three steps: 1) ungauging, 2) finite-depth local unitary (FDLU) circuit in a simpler TO, and 3) regauging~\cite{lyons-2025}. For the ungauging step, it is a sequential unitary circuit that ``unzips" along the length of the ribbon to a simpler TO. Then, the FDLU circuit is either a string operator or symmetry fractionalization pattern in the simpler TO. Finally, the regauging circuit ``zips up" along the length of the ribbon back to the original TO.
L000872	
L000873	The unitary ribbon for a $[2]$ charge vacuum pair is given by:
L000874	\begin{equation}
L000875	   F^{(+, [2]); 0} = \sum_{j} F^{(+, [2]); 0,j}_{\rho} = \prod_{x \in \rho} \mathcal{Z}_x^{\prod_{g < x} Z_{g}}.
L000876	\end{equation}
L000877	Here $x, g$ are edges on the lattice along the ribbon $\rho$, and $g < x$ means that $g$ occurs before edge $x$, traveling along $\rho$ from the start of the ribbon to the end. The gate $\mathcal{Z}_x^{Z_g}$ is a controlled gate with the qutrit on edge $x$ as the target and the qubit on edge $g$ as the control:
L000878	\begin{equation}
L000879	    \mathcal{Z}_x^{Z_g} \ket{\mu^a}_x \ket{\sigma^{b}} = \omega^{(-1)^{b} a} \ket{\mu^a}_x  \ket{\sigma^b}, \quad a = 0, 1, 2, \quad b = 0, 1.
L000880	\end{equation}
L000881	There can also be multiple controls on some set of edges $\{g\}$:
L000882	\begin{equation}
L000883	    \mathcal{Z}_x^{\prod_g Z_g} \ket{\mu^a}_x \prod_g \ket{\sigma^{b_g}}_g = \omega^{(-1)^{\sum_{g} b_g} a} \ket{\mu^a}_x \prod_g \ket{\sigma^b}_g, \quad a = 0, 1, 2, \quad b_g = 0, 1.
L000884	\end{equation}
