L001498	\subsection{$W$-flux and non-contractible $Z$ projectors of \ds3}
L001499	The $W$-flux is the topological flux defined by the ordered group product along a closed loop, based at a fixed origin, that encircles an excitation. Fig.~\ref{fig:w-paths} enumerates the different paths used to define the
L001500	$W$-flux stabilizer $S_{W_p}$, each of which encloses exactly one plaquette $p$. The corresponding projector $W_p$ is defined as the projector onto the $+1$ eigenspace of $S_{W_p}$. Using steps analogous to those employed in deriving the plaquette operator $B_p$, the $W$-flux projector can also be written as
L001501	\begin{align}
L001502	    W_p = W^{\mathbb{Z}_2}_p \, W^{\mathbb{Z}_3}_p .
L001503	\end{align}
L001504	Here $W^{\mathbb{Z}_2}_p = B^{\mathbb{Z}_2}_p$ (as defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:deriv_p_sigma}) for the paths listed in Fig.~\ref{fig:w-paths}, while $W^{\mathbb{Z}_3}_p$ has a form analogous to $S^{\mathbb{Z}_3}_B$ and acts
L001505	non-trivially on all qudits along the loop.
L001506	
L001507	Correlators of the $W$-flux are obtained by forming products of stabilizers, for example $S_{W_{p_1}} S_{W_{p_2}}$, and projecting onto their joint $+1$ eigenspace, yielding the projector $\Pi_{W_{p_1} W_{p_2}}$. This projector similarly factorizes into independent $\ZZ_2$ and $\ZZ_3$ components.
L001508	\begin{align}
L001509	    \Pi_{W_{p_1}W_{p_2}}	= 	\Pi^{\ZZ_2}_{W_{p_1}W_{p_2}} \Pi^{\ZZ_3}_{W_{p_1}W_{p_2}}
L001510	\end{align}
L001511	
L001512	\begin{figure}[!h]
L001513	    \centering
L001514	    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{figures_appendix/w-paths.pdf}
L001515	    \caption{\textbf{$W$-flux.} Paths along which $W$-flux stabilizers are evaluated to benchmark the			experimental results presented in the main text.}
