L001526	\section{Coherent moving of non-Abelian anyons: theory}\label{app:coherent-moving}
L001527	
L001528	In our experiment, we only need to coherently move non-Abelian pure fluxes. We will focus on the protocol for coherently moving non-Abelian flux for the rest of the section. This is a special case of the general procedure of coherent moving~\cite{lo_coherent}.
L001529	
L001530	Suppose we want to coherently move an anyon from location $A$ to $B$. Operationally, we demand a deterministic process such that there is no remnant anyon at the site $A$ after coherent moving. We implement coherent moving unitarily with the use of one ancilla qudit, where $d$ matches the quantum dimension of the non-Abelian anyon.
L001531	
L001532	Ribbon operators create pairs of excitation at their end points; to ensure that an anyon is deterministically moved from the starting site to the ending site, we need to ensure that the fusion outcome is always vacuum at the starting site. This is done by judicious choice of internal state at the ends of a ribbon operator. For a pair of non-Abelian fluxes, the state in the vacuum fusion channel is the symmetric superposition over all representatives in a conjugacy class $C$, i.e.,
L001533	\begin{equation}
L001534	    \ket{\text{vac}} =\frac{1}{|C|} \sum_{c\in \mathcal{C}}  \ket{c,\bar c}.
L001535	\end{equation}
L001536	
L001537	The pairing of $c,\bar c$ ensures flux neutrality since the total flux is $c\cdot \bar c = e$; the symmetrization ensures charge neutrality as any permutation of the terms leave the state invariant, so the state is not charged under any symmetry action (which acts by conjugation). For moving non-Abelian \textit{pure} flux, the procedure is simplified because pure fluxes already satisfy the property that the local internal flux state is a symmetric superposition over all possible basis states. Therefore, we only need to ensure the correct pairing $c,\bar c$ for the internal state of the ribbon operators.
L001538	
L001539	The coherent moving protocol of non-Abelian flux consists of three main steps:
L001540	\begin{enumerate}
L001541	    \item entangle the ancilla with the local internal state of the flux;
L001542	    \item conditioned on the ancilla, apply the corresponding ribbon operator;
L001543	    \item disentangle ancilla.
L001544	\end{enumerate}
L001545	
L001546	For step 1, a $C_L\mathcal{X}_a$ gate is implemented to entangle the ancilla with the local internal state of the flux.
L001547	
L001548	For step 2, a $C_2$ ribbon operator is conjugated by $C_a\mathcal{X}_{dir}$, where $dir$ is the first direct edge of the ribbon operator, such that starting local internal state created by the ribbon operator matches with the ancilla state. This ensures that the ribbon operator create the same $C_2$ flux at the starting site to cancel out the flux there.
L001549	
L001550	For step 3, we want to implement a $C_L\mathcal{X}_a$ gate again to disentangling the ancilla from the local internal state of the flux at the start. However, the flux at the start is annihilated to vacuum! The key is to recognize that the flux label \textit{based at the same vertex} remains the same due to the fact that ribbon operator can only create vacuum pair of anyons, i.e. ribbon operator satisfies the neutrality condition by construction~\cite{lo_universal_2025}. Therefore, the control qutrit is the global internal state (based at the starting vertex of the ribbon operator) of the new $C_2$ flux at the ending site.
L001551	
L001552	See Section~\ref{app:pullthrough-imple} and Section~\ref{app:xmeasure-imple} for examples of coherent moving circuit implementations.
L001553	
L001554	\section{Physical encoding and implementation of primitive gates for \ds3}\label{app:s3_encoding_and_circs}
L001555	This Section describes our implementation of qudit encoding and gates, realized with the native gate set of Quantinuum H2-series devices \cite{h2-data-sheet, noauthor_quantinuum_2024}. The native gates for the H2 architecture are as follows:
L001556	\begin{equation}
L001557	    \label{eq:1qgates}
L001558	    U_{1q}(\alpha,\beta) = e^{-\frac{1}{2}i\pi \beta Z} \ e^{-\frac{1}{2}i\pi \alpha X} \ e^{+\frac{1}{2}i\pi \beta Z}  ,\quad
L001559	
L001560	    R_Z(\alpha) = e^{-\frac{1}{2}i\pi\theta Z}\quad  \text{and}
L001561	\end{equation}
L001562	\begin{equation}
L001563	    \label{eq:ZZphase}
L001564	    \textrm{ZZPhase}(\theta) = e^{-\frac{1}{2}i\pi\theta (Z\otimes Z)}.
L001565	\end{equation}
L001566	The number of ZZPhase gates significantly affects the overall implementation cost and serves as a key metric for evaluating gate decompositions.
L001567	
L001568	Each six-dimensional qudit, representing the \ds3 degrees of freedom on the edges of our square lattice, is encoded using a  qutrit and a qubit.  The qutrit, in turn, is encoded using two qubits. Therefore, each qudit requires a total of 3 qubits. The qutrit encoding is defined as:
L001569	\begin{align}
L001570	    \label{eq:encoding}
L001571	    \ket{0}_{\blacktriangle}:=\ket{00}, \quad
L001572	    \ket{1}_{\blacktriangle}:=\ket{10}, \quad
L001573	    \ket{2}_{\blacktriangle}:=\ket{11}, \quad
L001574	    \ket{\textrm{nc}} := |01\rangle.
L001575	\end{align}
L001576	The non-computational state,  $|\text{nc}\rangle$, is used for error detection; its presence indicates that the two encoding qubits have fallen outside the qutrit subspace.
L001577	
L001578	Qutrits are primarily manipulated using the $\mathcal{X}$, $\mathcal{Z}$, and $\mathcal{C}$ gates, as defined in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:x_and_z_and_C_def}. $\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{Z}$ satisfy the commutation relation $\mathcal{XZ} = \omega \mathcal{ZX}$. The charge-conjugation gate $\mathcal{C}$ is Hermitian, $\mathcal{C}^\dagger=\mathcal{C}$, and conjugates $\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{Z}$:
L001579	\begin{align}
L001580	    \label{eq:cc_transform}
L001581	    \mathcal{C}\mathcal{X} \mathcal{C} = \mathcal{X}^\dagger\quad \mathrm{and} \quad
L001582	    \mathcal{C}\mathcal{Z} \mathcal{C} = \mathcal{Z}^\dagger.
L001583	\end{align}
L001584	
L001585	The qutrit $\mathcal{Z}$ gate can be efficiently implemented using two single-qubit native $R_Z$ gates:
L001586	\begin{align}
L001587	    \label{eq:Z}
L001588	    \mathcal{Z} &:=
L001589	    \cbox{5}{circs_primitive/Z.pdf}
L001590	    = \bar \omega\ \textrm{diag}(1,\ \omega,\ \omega,\ \bar \omega).
L001591	\end{align}
L001592	The action of $\mathcal{Z}$ on $|\textrm{nc}\rangle$ is trivial (up to $\omega$ phase). The extra $\bar \omega$ phase needs to be corrected to properly implement a controlled-$\mathcal{Z}$ (C$\mathcal{Z}$) gate.
L001593	In contrast, implementing $\mathcal{X}$ requires either two CNOT gates or a single ZZPhase gate:
L001594	\begin{align}
L001595	    \label{eq:qutrit-Z}
L001596	    \mathcal{X} &:= \cbox{30}{circs_primitive/X.pdf}
L001597	    = \begin{pmatrix}
L001598	        0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\
L001599	        0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\
L001600	        1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\
L001601	        0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\
L001602	    \end{pmatrix}
L001603	\end{align}
L001604	The charge-conjugation gate $\mathcal{C}$ can also be implemented using only a single ZZPhase gate.
L001605	\begin{align}
L001606	    \label{eq:qutrit-C}
L001607	    \mathcal{C} &:=
L001608	    \cbox{28}{circs_primitive/C.pdf}
L001609	    =  \begin{pmatrix}
L001610	        1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\
L001611	        0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\
L001612	        0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\
L001613	        0 & 0 & 1 & 0
L001614	    \end{pmatrix}
L001615	\end{align}
L001616	$\mathcal{X}$ can be transformed into $\mathcal{Z}$ gate by performing a qutrit Fourier transform, denoted by $\mathcal{H}$
L001617	which acts on the qutrit space as follows:
L001618	\begin{align}
L001619	    \label{eq:qutrit-H}
L001620	    \mathcal{H} \ket{i}_\blacktriangle = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} (\ket{0}_\blacktriangle + \omega^i \ket{1}_\blacktriangle + \omega^{2i} \ket{2}_\blacktriangle),
L001621	\end{align}
L001622	The native implementation of $\mathcal{H}$ requires three ZZPhase gates, preserving the $\ket{\textrm{nc}}$ state.
L001623	Note that we treat the action of $\mathcal{H}$ on $\ket{0}_{\blacktriangle}$ as a \emph{state preparation procedure}, which can be implemented using one ZZPhase gate:
L001624	\begin{align}
L001625	    \label{eq:state_prep}
L001626	    \mathcal{H}\ket{00} &=
L001627	    \cbox{40}{circs_primitive/state_prep.pdf}.
L001628	\end{align}
L001629	Furthermore, $\mathcal{H}$ commutes with $\mathcal{C}$, since $\mathcal{H}^2=\mathcal{C}$.
L001630	\\
L001631	
L001632	Similarly, $H_{12}$ diagonalizes $\mathcal{C}$, leaving $\ket{0}_\blacktriangle$ unchanged while applying a ``Hadamard gate'' to both $\ket{1}_\blacktriangle$ and $\ket{2}_\blacktriangle$.  In terms of native gates, $H_{12}$ requires a single ZZPhase gate:
L001633	\begin{align}
L001634	    \label{eq:qutrit-hc}
L001635	    H_{12} &:=
L001636	    \cbox{28}{circs_primitive/hc.pdf}
L001637	    =  \begin{pmatrix}
L001638	        1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\
L001639	        0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\
L001640	        0 & 0 & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\\
L001641	        0 & 0 & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
L001642	    \end{pmatrix}.
L001643	\end{align}
L001644	Since $\mathcal{C}$ squares to the identity, the C$\mathcal{C}$ gate is defined with a qubit control and qutrit target. To avoid applying three qubit CC$Z$ gate to implement C$\mathcal{C}$, we use the fact that $H_\mathcal{C}$ disentangles the $\mathcal{C}$ gate, i.e., $H_{12}\ \mathcal{C}\ H_{12}^\dagger = IZ$. The implementation of C$\mathcal{C}$ requires three ZZPhase gates:
L001645	\begin{align}
L001646	    \label{eq:CC}
L001647	    \text{C}\mathcal{C} &:=
L001648	    \cbox{16}{circs_primitive/cc.pdf}
L001649	\end{align}
L001650	The application of C$\mathcal{Z}$ requires four C$R_z$ gates:
L001651	\begin{align}
L001652	    \label{eq:qutrit-CZ}
L001653	    \textrm{C}\mathcal{Z} &:=
L001654	    \cbox{28}{circs_primitive/CZ.pdf},
L001655	\end{align}
L001656	where each of the C$R_z$ gate can be implemented using a single ZZPhase gate. The initial single qubit $R_z$ gates on control qutrit compensate for the extra phase $\bar \omega$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:qutrit-Z}. Applying C$\mathcal{Z}$ while transforming the target qutrit by $\mathcal{H}$ yields the C$\mathcal{X}$ gate:
L001657	\begin{align}
L001658	    \label{eq:qutrit-CX}
L001659	    \textrm{C}\mathcal{X} &:=
L001660	    \cbox{9}{circs_primitive/CX.pdf}
L001661	\end{align}
L001662	The C$\mathcal{X}$ gate requires a total of $3+4+3=10$ ZZPhase gates.
L001663	
L001664	We define a $\textrm{C}_2\textrm{NOT}$ gate with a qutrit as the control and a qubit as the target.
L001665	\begin{align}
L001666	    \label{eq:qubit-CNOT}
L001667	    \textrm{C}_2\textrm{NOT} &:=
L001668	    \cbox{13}{circs_primitive/qubitCNOT.pdf}
L001669	\end{align}
L001670	It acts trivially on the target qubit when the control qutrit is in the $\ket{0}$ or $\ket{1}$ state, and flips the target qubit when the control is in the $\ket{2}$ state. It is implemented by a single CNOT gate conditioned on the second physical qubit of the control qutrit, because in the physical encoding of the qutrit (see Eq.~\eqref{eq:encoding}), the second physical qubit is in the $\ket{1}$ state only for the $\ket{2} = \ket{11}$ qutrit state.
L001671	
L001672	Moreover, we define the C$X_{12}$ gate with a qutrit control and a qutrit target.
L001673	\begin{align}
L001674	    \label{eq:CX12}
L001675	    \textrm{C}X_{12} &:=
L001676	    \cbox{18}{circs_primitive/CX12.pdf}
L001677	\end{align}
L001678	The gate acts as the identity when the control qutrit is in the $\ket{0}$ or $\ket{1}$ state. When the control is in the $\ket{2}$ state, it swaps the target states $\ket{1}$ and $\ket{2}$ while leaving $\ket{0}$ invariant.
